today we are looking at centripetal  force this is one of the more unusual  forces that we see in physics and it's  almost always dealt with or comes up in  relation to circular motion so the first  thing we need to do is understand what  takes place during circular motion to  cause the actual motion to occur so the  first place we could consider this is in  relation to a satellite going around the  planet Earth so if we draw our planet  Earth there and we have our satellite  the satellite is moving at a velocity  which if we were drawing a force era  would take it in a straight direction  but then to stop it flying off into  space we have gravity that pulls it  straight towards the earth the resultant  force of these two takes the satellite  in a slightly less than straight off  direction it goes towards the earth if  we keep doing this and keep drawing our  satellite and each time we have this  arrow that's just going a little bit  off-center what we end up with is a  series of joined up arrows that if we  made them completely tiny would make up  the orbit that you see the satellite  going on so we have two components to  our circular motion we have gravity and  velocity as the forces involved now we  need to consider velocity and its parts  in its definition so velocity is a speed  in a direction and if we have a change  in velocity we have what we call  acceleration  now initially when we look at our  satellite we would say that it has the  same speed so how can it possibly have  an acceleration well we must remember  that velocity is made up of two key  parts it is speed in a direction and in  the case of our satellite the speed is  constant but the direction is changing  if we have a change in direction our  velocity is actually changing which  means we have acceleration that  acceleration can only be caused by a  force any change within our movement  must be caused by a force this force in  this case is causing our object to move  towards the center so we call it the  centripetal force and in the case of our  satellite that force is gravity so in  our current situation of a satellite  going around the planet we have a  centripetal force acting and the name of  that centripetal force in this case is  gravity now that is not the only force  that can act as a centripetal force we  have another two so in total we have  three common centripetal forces we have  gravity which is the most often quoted  in exams then we also have friction and  we have tension to give an example in  each case firstly gravity we've already  looked at that with satellites if we  want to look at friction we can consider  the bend on a road and a car going round  that bend as the car goes around the  bend its force and its direction of  movement wants it to go straight on  however the car because of its  grip on the road manages to turn around  that road that grip is a result of  friction so that friction is enabling  the car to turn the corner which means  that it is friction in this case which  is the centripetal force if we consider  tension we could consider a  merry-go-round with children playing on  it and when the children play on the  merry-go-round they may well decide to  hold on to the bar at the center as they  hold on to the bar at the center they  feel a force of tension as they move  around that tension in their arms is in  this case the centripetal force so we  have their three different examples of  centripetal force in action  so in summary centripetal force is a  force that acts towards the center  during circular motion it acts because  that although the velocity might be  constant there is within velocity a  change in direction which means a change  that leads to acceleration the  acceleration needs a force to act and  the three most common forces that we see  acting as a centripetal force are  gravity friction and tension
 

